    {
      "type": "teaser",
      "visible": true,
      "title": "",
      "content": {
        "media": [
          {
            "type": "image",
            "src": "assets/img-000.jpg",
            "caption": "**GARD** restores degraded multi-view inputs jointly in geometry-aware feature space, recovering accurate 3D point clouds and high-quality RGB images simultaneously.",
            "alt": "Figure 1 — GARD framework: degraded views and depths (left) are transformed into restored views, depths, and a clean 3D point cloud (right)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "id": "bacc49bd"
    },
    {
      "type": "tldr",
      "visible": true,
      "title": "TL;DR",
      "content": {
        "text": "We propose **~#a51fb7:Geometry-Aware Representation Denoising (GARD)~**, a novel framework that performs diffusion-based multi-view restoration directly in the feature space of a feed-forward 3D reconstruction model, enabling **~#a51fb7:simultaneous recovery of 3D scene geometry and high-quality RGB images~** from degraded multi-view inputs. Our method outperforms existing pixel-space and VAE-based latent-space restoration approaches, demonstrating the effectiveness of geometry-aware feature denoising for robust multi-view 3D reconstruction."
      },
      "id": "30d2c5fe"
    },
    {
      "id": "29819027",
      "type": "spacer",
      "visible": true,
      "content": {
        "height": 69
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "2062a193",
      "type": "image",
      "visible": true,
      "content": {
        "media": [
          {
            "type": "image",
            "src": "assets/img-001.jpg",
            "caption": "",
            "alt": ""
          }
        ]
      },
      "title": "Pixel-Space and VAE-Based Restoration Fall Short for 3D Reconstruction",
      "sectionLabel": "MOTIVATION"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "visible": true,
      "content": {
        "text": "Feed-forward 3D reconstruction models perform well under clean conditions, but fail under real-world degradations such as motion blur.\n\n\n**Where should restoration happen?**\n\n⚠️ **~#a51fb7:Pixel-space restoration~**: Applying a pixel-space denoiser independently to each view fails to leverage multi-view information and cannot enforce cross-view geometry consistency during restoration. This often leads to view-dependent artifacts and inconsistencies that propagate to the reconstructor, resulting in impaired geometry estimation performance.\n\n⚠️ **~#a51fb7:Multi-view restoration in VAE-based latent space~**: Restoration in compressed VAE-based latent space can introduce an information bottleneck, discarding fine-grained details and high-frequency structures essential for accurate multi-view correspondence and 3D reconstruction.\n\n 💡 **~#a51fb7:GARD~**: Perform denoising directly in the **geometry-aware feature space** of a feed-forward reconstructor, preserving both cross-view consistency and visual detail, making it more suitable for restoration than pixel space or compressed VAE latents.",
        "bulletItems": []
      },
      "id": "abd4bf96"
    },
    {
      "id": "616c1c0a",
      "type": "spacer",
      "visible": true,
      "content": {
        "height": 70
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "visible": true,
      "sectionLabel": "METHOD",
      "title": "Overall Framework",
      "content": {
        "media": [
          {
            "type": "image",
            "src": "assets/img-002.jpg",
            "caption": "The GARD denoiser is inserted at layer K of a frozen multi-view encoder, restoring degraded representations before they propagate to geometry and RGB decoders.",
            "alt": "Figure 3 — GARD framework overview: (a) denoiser inserted into frozen multi-view encoder, (b) training with interpolated flow matching + attention alignment loss, (c) multi-view DDT encoder-decoder architecture with global and frame attention layers."
          }
        ]
      },
      "id": "4f35cc8c",
      "paddingBottom": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "9e8dddcd",
      "type": "text",
      "visible": true,
      "content": {
        "text": "(a) **~#a51fb7:GARD Framework~**: The GARD denoiser is **learned within the representation space of a frozen multi-view encoder to restore degraded intermediate representations into restored representations** before they are propagated through the remaining encoder layers. The restored representations are then decoded by their respective decoders to produce geometry predictions and restored RGB images.\n\n(b) **~#a51fb7:GARD Denoiser Training~**: The GARD denoiser is optimized using an **interpolated flow matching loss** together with an **attention alignment loss**, which jointly learns the mapping from degraded to clean feature representations while preserving geometric consistency through explicit alignment of attention maps.\n\n(c) **~#a51fb7:GARD Denoiser Architecture~**: The GARD denoiser adopts a multi-view latent diffusion architecture, comprising a **DDT encoder and a DDT wide decoder, with global attention layers inserted to enable multi-view modeling**, thereby facilitating global context aggregation and reconstruction of high-dimensional multi-view representations.",
        "bulletItems": []
      },
      "paddingBottom": 16
    },
    {
      "id": "0652b08c",
      "type": "spacer",
      "visible": true,
      "content": {
        "height": 70
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ff343900",
      "type": "image",
      "visible": true,
      "content": {
        "media": [
          {
            "type": "image",
            "src": "assets/img-003.png",
            "caption": "(a) PCK performance on high-quality (HQ) input images.   (b) PCK performance under progressively increasing levels of degradation (mild, moderate, and severe), demonstrating robustness to input corruption.",
            "alt": ""
          }
        ]
      },
      "title": "Geometry-Aware Feature Analysis",
      "sectionLabel": "METHOD"
    },
    {
      "id": "31c67c48",
      "type": "text",
      "visible": true,
      "content": {
        "text": "We evaluate the PCK accuracy of three feature cost volumes under two experimental settings to validate the effectiveness of our proposed denoising space. **DA3 features preserve geometric structure more effectively than VAE and DINOv2**, achieving higher keypoint correspondence accuracy and stronger robustness to degradation, making them well-suited for representation-level denoising and downstream 3D reconstruction.\n\n"
      },
      "sectionLabel": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "cbc716c8",
      "type": "image",
      "visible": true,
      "content": {
        "media": [
          {
            "type": "image",
            "src": "assets/img-004.png",
            "caption": "Cross-view correspondence visualization of feature cost volumes constructed from VAE, DINOv2, and DA3 feature cost volumes  under (a) clean high-quality (HQ) multi-view inputs and (b) progressively increasing degradation levels of degraded multi-view inputs (mild, moderate, and heavy).  ",
            "alt": ""
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "379e2320",
      "type": "text",
      "visible": true,
      "content": {
        "text": "",
        "bulletItems": [
          "**~#a51fb7:DA3~**: Produce the sharpest and most geometrically consistent correspondences across views under both clean and degraded conditions.",
          "**~#a51fb7:DINOv2~**: Achieves reasonably accurate matching with less precise and less stable response regions.",
          "**~#a51fb7:VAE~**: Exhibit highly scattered and ambiguous correspondences under both clean and degraded conditions, reflecting limited geometry-aware representation quality caused by heavy latent compression."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "4740dbf6",
      "type": "callout",
      "visible": true,
      "content": {
        "text": "**~#a51fb7:DA3 > DINOv2 ≫ VAE~** in correspondence accuracy and robustness, maintaining strong geometric consistency as degradation increases.",
        "calloutIcon": "💡"
      }
    },
